<Base title="Why Kota" description="Kota already has the physics talent quantum needs. The case for making Kota India's next quantum city.">
  <section class="article">
    <div class="narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">Why Kota</p>
      <h1>Quantum needs physics talent. Kota already has it.</h1>
      <p class="desc">Every year, Kota trains a huge share of India's brightest physics and maths students. Then almost all of them leave. Kota Quantum Valley asks a simple question: what if the jobs came here instead?</p>

      <h2>What Kota already has</h2>
      <div class="grid">
        <div class="card"><span class="num">01</span><h3>Talent</h3><p>Lakhs of students who have mastered physics and maths.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><span class="num">02</span><h3>Teachers</h3><p>A city of people who explain hard science for a living.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><span class="num">03</span><h3>Lower costs</h3><p>Cheaper to live and build than the big metros.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><span class="num">04</span><h3>A ready city</h3><p>Hostels, transport and a young crowd already in place.</p></div>
      </div>

      <h2>Why now</h2>
      <p><strong>India:</strong> the National Quantum Mission was approved with a budget of about ₹6,000 crore for 2023–24 to 2030–31.</p>
      <p><strong>Andhra Pradesh:</strong> the Amaravati Quantum Valley is coming up on a 50-acre campus, with IBM, TCS and L&amp;T as key partners.</p>
      <p><strong>Rajasthan:</strong> the state approved its Semiconductor Policy 2026 in March 2026. A state quantum plan doesn't exist yet — that's the gap.</p>

      <h2>What Kota needs</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>A Rajasthan state quantum mission, with Kota named as a hub.</li>
        <li>A quantum lab at RTU or IIIT Kota, open to students.</li>
        <li>Quantum skilling programs built with Kota's coaching ecosystem.</li>
        <li>An incubator for deep-tech startups that want to stay in Kota.</li>
      </ol>

